# ec/tools/check_py_citations.py, wired into the cheap gate.
# Prepared here rather than landed, for the reason
# docs/ci/agent-gates-cross-decoder-disagreement.patch gives:
# `.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh` is copied from the agent-pipeline template
# and the pipeline's push token has no `workflow` scope, so a branch editing it
# fails at the end of the PR rather than at the start of it.
#
# To land it:
#
#   git apply docs/ci/agent-gates-py-source-citations.patch
#
# and that is the whole change.
#
# What it buys. The committed Python sources cite line numbers inside
# ec/tools/xdata_register_map.py -- `inc_dptr_sites.py`'s pair fold,
# `test_xdata_cluster_names.py`'s `--no-eq-guard` pointers,
# `test_xdata_register_map.py`'s `list(...)` defaults and its refusals,
# `test_xdata_guard_off_row_join.py`'s `cluster_key` hash. On `ad595588` every
# one of them named the wrong code, and a `:NNN` that resolves to a different
# statement is not a broken link: it is a sentence that reads exactly as well
# with the wrong number as with the right one, and nothing else in the tree is
# red over it. The checker resolves each anchor as a string of the tool's own
# source and holds each citation to the line that anchor is on today, so the
# next edit to that tool -- and it is edited often -- is a red row carrying the
# right number beside the wrong one rather than a sentence that quietly means
# something else.
#
# Cheap tier by what it reads: the tool, and the files that declare a citation
# into it. All committed text. No firmware image, no Ghidra, no network, no
# assembler, no PyYAML.
#
# **Placement is the whole design problem here, and it was measured rather than
# chosen.** This file is the most contended surface in the repository: the
# prepared patches in docs/ci/ have hunks in it, and the obvious spots are each
# one of theirs' context -- the `gate` list is claimed line by line, and so is
# check_ghidra_tooling()'s body and check_doc_links()'s. Sweeping every
# (definition site, call site) pair in the file against what
# tools/test_agent_gates_patches.py requires of a prepared patch -- applies
# alone, composes with each prepared patch in either order, and the whole set
# still lands and parses -- is what put both ends where they are: the definition
# above the comment that documents scan_refs, and the call on the `local out`
# line inside check_scan_refs_smoke_test. That suite's composition cases apply
# the whole set in every order and assert each one still applies; that is what
# proves this, rather than this header asserting it.
#
# The call returns early rather than folding its verdict into that function's
# trailing `grep -q`, because the tail is testdata-row-claims' context and
# widening the hunk to it is the difference between a patch that composes and
# one that does not. A citation failure therefore reads as a red
# `scan_refs.py smoke test` gate -- the cheap tier, which is where this is --
# rather than as a scan_refs failure, which is what it is not.
#
# **Until a human lands this, nothing runs it per commit, and a PR that breaks
# one of these citations merges green.** The suite behind it is
# ec/tools/test_check_py_source_citations.py, which tools/run-tests.sh finds on
# its own; this patch does not gate that either, which is the whole reason this
# file exists. The write-up is docs/findings/py-source-citations.md.
#
# Cut whole against the committed `.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh` on 2026-10-04,
# issue #1128, from a scratch clone so the hunk is what `git apply` will accept
# rather than a transcription, and shellcheck-clean in the state it produces. It
# carries an `index` line for the same reason
# docs/ci/agent-gates-0751-self-test.patch does.
#
diff --git a/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh b/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
index 16c70c3..0f1b71f 100755
--- a/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
+++ b/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
@@ -65,12 +65,36 @@ sys.exit(0 if ok else 1)
 PY
 }
 
+# The line citations the committed Python sources make into
+# xdata_register_map.py, held to the code those citations name. A `:NNN` that
+# has drifted onto a different statement is not a broken link: it is a sentence
+# that reads exactly as well with the wrong number as with the right one, and
+# nothing else in the tree is red over it.
+#
+# It is a function of its own rather than an entry in the cheap tier's `gate`
+# list because that list is fully claimed: every line of it is another prepared
+# patch's context in docs/ci/, and an insertion there stops that patch applying
+# -- which tools/test_agent_gates_patches.py holds, so an insertion composed
+# against no patch but this one is a red run and not a wiring. The call is the
+# same argument one function over: check_ghidra_tooling()'s body is claimed end
+# to end, as is check_doc_links()'s.
+#
+# It reads committed text only -- the tool, and the files that declare a
+# citation into it -- so it is cheap-tier work by the split above. No firmware
+# image, no Ghidra, no network, no assembler.
+check_py_citations() {
+  local rc=0
+  python3 ec/tools/check_py_citations.py || rc=1
+  return "$rc"
+}
+
 # Not a style check -- ec/tools/scan_refs.py producing the wrong count
 # silently would mislead every future reference to it. Cross-checks
 # against a value docs/findings.md and registers.yaml both cite, so a
 # regression here is a regression a reader would also hit.
 check_scan_refs_smoke_test() {
   local out
+  check_py_citations || return 1
   out=$(python3 ec/tools/scan_refs.py ec/firmware/GMxMGxx_11.800 0x043E) || return 1
   echo "$out"
   echo "$out" | grep -q 'refs=15' && echo "$out" | grep -q 'referenced'
